      "id": "7yrlrqs",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Background of Fundamental Duties"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Fundamental Duties were incorporated into Part IV-A of the Constitution under Article 51A by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1976, following recommendations of the Swaran Singh Committee.",
        "Originally containing 10 duties, an 11th duty regarding compulsory education for children aged 6 to 14 was added by the 86th Constitutional Amendment Act in 2002.",
        "Fundamental Duties serve as a constant reminder to citizens that while enjoying rights, they must also perform duties towards the nation and society.",
        "These duties are non-justiciable in nature, meaning they cannot be directly enforced through courts unless supported by parliamentary law.",
        "Duties are divided into moral duties (such as cherishing freedom struggle ideals) and civic duties (such as respecting the National Flag and National Anthem)."
      ],
      "id": "1dg9bhb"
    },
